      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this one. A single dose of 50 mg exceeds the maximum labeled dose by 5\u00d7, and it has no therapeutic benefit over 10 mg. It carries a real risk of profound hypotension and shock, and of non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema. Amlodipine's long half-life means the effects would last a long time and be hard to reverse. You also haven't given a clinical reason for the dose, so I can't treat it as intended therapy.\n\nI can do either of these right away:\n- Enter **10 mg** by mouth once daily, the maximum standard dose.\n- Enter **5 mg** by mouth once daily, the usual starting dose, with a note to titrate.\n\nIf you think this patient needs more blood pressure control than 10 mg gives, adding a second agent such as an ACE inhibitor, ARB, or thiazide is the usual approach. A pharmacist or your attending can help you pick one. Tell me which option you want and I'll enter it."
      }
